    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce20f0e9_95fa03bb",
        "filename": "net/xfrm/xfrm_state.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1398,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-08T01:15:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s a really tricky, but yes I believe I need to nest the locks since the SA shouldn\u0027t be allowed to change state during the update, and they need to be in this order to avoid a deadlock due to the locking order of the timer expiry function (first lock the sa, then lock the xfrm_state_lock).\n\nIf you go look at some slightly older versions of the patch set, I had tried to refactor this and clean it up, but it used the opposite locking order, which made it less feasible.\n\nThere are relatively few places that actually take the SA lock rather than the xfrm_state_lock, but take a look at xfrm_timer_handler(): the entire function is locked by x-\u003elock, and it calls __xfrm_state_delete(), which in turn locks xfrm_state_lock.",
      "parentUuid": "69de85eb_456c0f21",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1398,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 1398,
        "endChar": 14
      },
